From d88226bc2a7d309fc7eff11cc48ffbc6b393fdc4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kakarin <27130590+kakarin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 01:18:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] feat(auth): let the owner pin a custom access token
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The standing credential was always 192 generated bits, so the only way to get
in from a phone was to copy it or scan an invite. Add a second path: set the
token yourself.

- config `accessToken` (8-128 printable ASCII, empty = generated). Applied at
  load; a value that differs from the stored token revokes every device session
  because sessions are independent of the token.
- `POST /dsh-reverse-proxy/set-token` + a Custom token field in Settings →
  Reverse proxy. Applies live: listener torn down and rebound in place, tunnel
  restarted when live, same revocation. Rejections answer 400 with a stable
  code so the panel can localise them without parsing prose.
- fix(persist): the state reader dropped any token shorter than 24 characters,
  so an owner-chosen token was persisted and then silently replaced on the next
  start, taking its device sessions with it. Read-back now accepts exactly what
  the validator accepts.

Tests: validation bounds, config pinning, live set, unchanged resubmit, every
rejection code, persistence round-trip, session revocation, and the
read-back/hydrate regression that motivated the persist fix.
